\f4\fs26 Although she had spent her career unraveling mysteries far more puzzling and inexplicable than her present mundane missing persons case. As with every other inquiry, she would attack with finesse, bringing to bear the adroit artifices born of experience and skill. Missing persons investigations necessarily involve interviewing relatives of the missing as, more often than not, a relative or friend was involved at some level. Guilty family members are always eager to lend a hand, but Hayley Lanning was never fooled, her legendary interviewing expertise would always get to the truth, no matter how awful. \
A lone visit to Emil\'92s family home in The Pale was a frightening prospect. Her interviewing skills didn\'92t mean shit down there and she would be in mortal danger. The Pale was filled with the refuse of the Primary Trade Dominion who would have no problem in raping and murdering an unaccompanied female, police officer or not. \
Hayley approached the door of the Kalheri hovel with trepidation, stepping over a dirty, dark haired man sleeping on the cold, filthy concrete. The door was a soiled translucent polymer with a small clear plastic window allowing the occupant an opportunity to identify assailants before the door is kicked in. On this occasion, however, this visitor did not force entry, but stood quietly at the door and politely waited. \
\'93Hello? Mrs. Kalheri? I am Det. Supt Lanning. Can I come in?\'94 She did not want to enter the domicile. It was impossible to tell what sort of disgusting vermin riddled the premises and she was not keen to find out. She had burned many an outfit after visiting the pale, as it was near impossible to get rid of either the lice or the odour. She pushed and the door creaked open to revealed garbage and refuse piled high with a small pathway dug through to each room.\
She entered and fully imbibed the fragrance of rotting food, mildew and sundry filth causing her breakfast to quickly rise and fill her mouth. She looked left and right and then just emptied her stomach contents onto one of the piles and then on the floor. Breathing through her mouth she noticed, with some disgust, that she could still unmistakably taste the unpleasant air of the squalid house. She stepped carefully through the refuse to what might be described as a lounge room, it was bigger that the other rooms and seemed to have some chairs that were not totally smothered. It was in the kitchen where she made the gristly discovery, an adult female and two female children, dead on the floor, with massive sealed wounds, characteristic of a particle weapon used at close range. \
Signs of lividity indicated the bodies had not been moved after death and they had been there for at least twelve hours. Det Lanning confirmed the estimate by comparing the ambient temperature with that of the bodies. She called it in and after waiting an eternity, a couple of uniforms wandered through the door. The death of an L.1 housewife was not important to the average cop, even an obvious murder such as this didn\'92t seem to raise too much interest in these guys. These two examples of law enforcement\'92s best were typical of many of her colleagues - cynical and lazy. They assume a world weary persona, having seen it all, but in reality they achieved fuck-all and complained at every opportunity. In particular they complained the law made their job too damned difficult and only corrupt sycophants get promoted. They were not wrong on either count, but what they could not or would not acknowledge is difficulty creates expertise and suck-ups always get promoted.\
   \
\'93I am Detectective Supt Lanning and I want this matter given the highest priority.\'94 She directed. \'93Full forensics, no short-cuts.\'94\
\'93Why? She\'92s just some low life. I mean just look at this shit-hole.\'94 The senior of the two said. \'93We don\'92t have the resource to investigate every murder.\'94\
\'93I am acting on a Commissioner\'92s Direction. Do not get in my way little men.\'94\
\'93Yes, ma\'92am. I\'92ll call in the Cavalry.\'94 He knew when to about-face.\
\'93I expect all information to be sent to me. You are now in charge of this crime scene. Don\'92t stuff it up, boys. I will be watching you.\'94\
\'93Whatever you want, Superintendent.\'94 The youngest patrolman was now is full suck-up mode and accompanied her to the front door and watched as she disappeared into The Pale. He turned back to his partner, \'93What a fucken bitch! Who the fuck does she this she is? Huh?\'94\
\'93Yeah, fuck her.\'94 The older one agreed, but after some contemplation, \'93Make the call. We had better keep the Commissioner happy.\'94\
* * *\
Hayley returned to her desk in the Commissioners Office and was very glad to be back in a clean environment. She did not like leg work, especially when it took her to the Pale. It was simply a disgusting place. For the modern detective, investigations are mostly talking on the vid-phone to interview witnesses or even suspects. How on earth anyone conducted an investigation before the invention of vid-phones Hayley could not understand. She found it impossible to comprehend how prehistoric investigators did their work. 
\f5\i Imagine speaking to every witness face to face. It must have taken forever!
\f4\i0 \
She then commenced her list of calls and was shortly speaking to Kathy of the Morgue. \'93Do you have any results on the samples?\'94\
\'93Hi Hayley. Wow, it was so lovely to meet you today! I have to report that the computer records are now missing. There is no record of any samples taken, not even the biopsy I took this morning.\'94 Kathy did not sound happy, but was sporting a gormless grin. \
\'93Can\'92t you get more samples?\'94\
\'93No, his wife collected him about an hour ago. The records disappeared a few minutes later.\'94 She let that sit for a second. \'93I did keep a small section of the tissue sample I took this morning, cool huh?\'94 She giggled. \'93I will conduct the tests myself, but it will take a while.\'94\uc0\u8232 \
\'93His wife took him? I just left his wife about half an hour ago and she was in no condition to collect anyone.\'94 Recalling the dreadful scene she recently stumbled upon. She could still smell the stench that had greeted her at the Kalheri residence and this thought had her abdomen lightly jerking as she suppressed nausea.\
\'93I\'92ve had our computer-techs take a look and they can\'92t find any evidence of tampering. Whoever has hacked our system here really knows what they are doing. This is no teenager on a joy ride through the link, this is serious and well organised. If you want to find your man Kalheri, you will have to sort out our data corruption problem.\'94\
\'93Get the techs to send me all the security files and vids since yesterday. In the meantime I will see if can get hold of someone who can help and I will get back to you.\'94 \
\'93Whoever it is they will have to be very good, Superintendent.\'94 Dr Logan\'92s hologram smiled brightly. \'93The techs at my end are no fools, but they are stumped.\'94\
\'93I will track down the best man for the job.\'94 She terminated the call and then raised the Commissioner\'92s secretary. \'93Janice, is this big man in?\'94\
\'93Yes.\'94\
\'93Good. Can you tell him I need to see him?\'94\
\'93What about? He is a busy man.\'94 She saw her job as protecting the commissioner from any distractions and garrisoned the terrain leading to his door.\
\'93None of your business, L2. Tell him I am on the way.\'94 \
Several minutes later she blew past the secretary, gave a cursory knock on the door and burst into the Commissioner\'92s Private Office. He was seated behind his desk reading text from his vid screen.\
\'93At least you knocked.\'94 Said the Commissioner, not unhappy. \'93What do you want?\'94\
\'93I am here to report that I have located Emil Kalheri, sir.\'94 Hayley announced in a grey monotone.\
\'93Good, good. Where is he?\'94\
\'93When I left him, he was lying on a slab in the Health, Relaxation and Respite Zone Morgue.\'94\
\'93Excellent work.\'94\
 \
\'93He has disappeared again. According to the Coroner\'92s Office, his wife collected his body about ten minutes ago.\'94\
\'93He has not disappeared then, he is with his family.\'94\
\'93No. I found Mrs. Kalheri and daughters dead on the floor of their home about an 2 hours ago. They had been dead for quite a while and there is no way she collected Emil\'92s body today. On top of that all of his records have been deleted.\'94\
\'93Like I said before, what do you want?\'94 Commissioner was getting a little testy. 
\f5\i I asked her to find a missing person not bitch about the computer network.
\f4\i0 \
\'93I need a computer geek, preferably an expert in cyber-security.\'94 Hayley announced. \'93We will have to put him on contract.\'94\
\'93We\'92ve got plenty of them in this department.\'94 Commissioner did not like bringing in outsiders, they were alway way too expensive and could never keep their expert mouths shut. \'93What\'92s wrong with our experts?\'94 \
\'93They are not good enough and they are, without exception, too lazy and too indiscreet. I need someone independent of this department.\'94 Detective Lanning did not like shining a light of truth on her department. She adored investigative work, but despised the lazy jury-fuckers she called colleagues, those who, either deliberately or by abject incompetence, made her job impossible. There were always some people who did not like to work, but law enforcement attracted more than their fair share, far more. \'93I need someone who not only knows what they are doing, but also cares about what they are doing. The Police Department is not exactly over run with these types.\'94\
\'93Someone you can trust?\'94 He joked. He trusted no-one, why should she?\
\'93I don\'92t think I can trust any outsider, just more than the members of this department. I need your permission for funding. He won\'92t come cheap.\'94 \
\'93Yes, but he will have to be operational. I am not paying contract rates for a fucking desk-jockey.\'94 The Commissioner chuckled, an untrained geek wouldn\'92t last too long in a critical incident and the department would not have to pay the contract out. He loved the idea of dropping a bucket of shit on an uninitiated outsider. \'93Go to it.\'94\
